## Verdict

**Agree** on cause, owner (the product's results pass and forward model, not the
worker) and the need for an operator scope grant. **Disagree** on which lever is
primary: I recommend the row-concurrent residual first and the concurrent profile
pass only as the fallback, the reverse of the post-mortem's ruling.

## Re-verified against current source

- `FittedProblems.fs:100-148`: `jacobian`, `report` and the private `sigmas` are plain
  members, so every `sigmaAt` evaluates the rank's Jacobian and `reportFrom`'s own
  one again; `quality` a third. Memoizing them is plainly right and changes no number.
- `FittedProblems.fs:227-243`: `pointsFrom` re-fits one point at a time, each told to
  the watch from `told.Value`; `FitResultsTests` pins those counts as one contiguous
  run.
- `StudyForward.fs:925-975` `residualWith` walks every run's rows serially through
  `predictedReadingOf`; one residual of AT-2's 44 configurations costs 1.31 s
  (probe), and every phase of the fit is made of residuals: the main solve (54
  iterations, 360 s), each Jacobian (15 columns, ~20 s) and every re-fit iteration
  (~35 s, 83 % of the profile pass in ALGLIB's numeric Jacobian).

## Why the residual first

- It is the only lever that shortens every phase. The concurrent profile pass leaves
  the 360 s main solve and ~400 s for the longest re-fit (6218 s / 24, plus its
  Jacobians), projecting ~800 s against a 900 s bound: the post-mortem's own medium
  confidence. Rows are independent Berreman solves; spread over the machine's 128
  logical processors, 44 rows bring a residual far below a second, and the whole fit,
  profiles still serial, into minutes.
- It keeps the vector bit-identical (each row deterministic, assembled in order), so
  every stored number stays the same, which 0438's byte comparison of the fit run
  needs; and AT-1's committed-kit equality (its two `QZ-LR` fit runs compared byte for
  byte) proves it on every kit-acceptance run.
- It changes no watch semantics: the profile counts stay contiguous and in order, so
  no `FitResultsTests` fact needs extending. The concurrent pass would need a new
  ordering contract for counts that the UI's progress reads.
- It also fixes the product: the app's own Fit of this study would otherwise stay
  above two hours.
- Its one cost is S11.2's "nothing after the first error is solved": rows after a
  refusal are now solved and dropped. The answer (the first refusal in row order) is
  unchanged; the ruling states it.

Risk: thread safety of the forward model. The post-mortem's grep found no
module-level cache or mutable state in the solver, Domain or Optimization; the
committed-kit byte comparison and `constructor-unit-tests` are the check.

## Recommendation

Option 1 reshaped, as ruling R0004 in `.manual/035-step-0436-study-fit-runtime-recovery.md`:
(1) a `FittedProblem` evaluates its Jacobian and report once (`FittedProblems.fs`);
(2) `residualWith` solves the rows of all runs concurrently and answers the same
vector, or the first refusal in row order (`StudyForward.fs`); (3) only if AT-2's fit
then measures above 900 s, the profile re-fits run concurrently (`FittedProblems.fs`,
`StudyEngine.fs`, with the named `FitResultsTests`/`StudyEngineTests` facts
extended). No solver, tolerance, threshold, start or stored number changes; the
measured AT-2 fit duration is recorded. Verify with the 26 gates of
`.slices/0436.gates` plus `constructor-unit-tests` as declared in `.slices/0426.gates`.
Forward: preserve at 0437-0494; 0460 (edits `StudyForward.fs`) keeps the concurrent
residual; 0476 (moves the offset frame) keeps one Jacobian and one report per problem.

Option 2 (raising the deadlines) is rejected as the post-mortem argues: the fit's
